In essence, the LE is the mass ratio of therapeutic agents that successfully conjugate with the nanocarriers during preparation procedure, which is mostly associated with the physicochemical properties of nanocarrier, initial mass of therapeutic agents, chemical interactions, and loading strategy (Table 1) [25,26]. Moreover, in order to optimize the performance of drug delivery and for better therapeutic effects, several parameters of nanocarriers need to be considered in each step of the preparation process. Recently, it has been demonstrated that, theoretical models can effectively simulate and predict physical properties of nanocarriers such as size, shape, and rigidity, which can further improve the overall therapeutic performance of drug delivery [27]. Other studies have reported that, the introduction of functional groups to a nanocarrier could change its interaction with loaded drugs and greatly impact the drug delivery behavior [28,29]. We believe that, several strategies should be developed to improve the drug delivery performance (Fig. 1), including a) performing theoretical investigation, simulating the physicochemical properties of nanocarriers and evaluation of their limitations before chemical synthesis, b) applying various modifications based on the types of nanocarrier to obtain a high drug loading, c) coating the surface of nanocarriers with biodegradable polymers to increase their blood half-life and accumulation at the TME, d) applying innovative strategies to

recognition of the targeted cancer cells and CSCs, and f) designing nanocarriers that are responsive to internal and external stimuli for controlled- and sustained- drug release at the desired locations.

# 2.1. Mesoporous silica nanoparticles (MSNs)

Mesoporous silica was first introduced by Mobile Oil Corporation in 1982 [30]. Since then it has gained more attention as a promising delivery carrier due to its favorable physicochemical characteristics, including high porosity [31], controllable surface area, easy surface modification, textural structure [32,33], good biocompatibility [34], and unique "on-off" property for drug release [35]. A series of gatekeepers which can operate in response to endogenous and exogenous stimuli, have been developed to control the release of different cargos (Fig. 2). These gatekeepers block the pore entrances and enable MSNs to release the entrapped cargos in the presence of certain triggers. These structures play a fundamental role in controlling drug release at the desired locations [36,37]. Designing MSNs functionalized with gatekeepers that rely on the inherent TME features and are degraded by the physiological signals such as changes in pH, redox reactions, some enzymes, glucose, and ATP have received intensive attention. The typical pH in TME has been recorded to be significantly lower than
