normal tissues [38,39]. Therefore, applying pH-sensitive gatekeepers is a promising strategy for intelligent drug release. In this sense, several types of pH-sensitive gatekeepers such as polyvinylpyrrolidone (PVP) [40], polyacrylic acid (PAA) [41], cyclodextrin (CD) [42], polydopamine (PDA) [43,44], \(\upbeta\)-cyclodextrin (\(\upbeta\)-CD) [45], gold NPs (AuNPs) [46,47], and chitosan [48,49] can be used which are decomposed in acidic environments to release therapeutic agents in a controlled manner. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that the expression and activity of glutathione (GSH), as a biological reducer agent in CRC tissues, is significantly greater than that in adjacent normal colorectal tissues [50]. Several studies have managed the drug release by developing endogenous redox-responsive gatekeepers based on CSU-clovable disulfide bonds [51,52,53]. Another type of endogenous stimulus has been developed based on various overexpressed enzymes in cancer tissues. Cathepsin B is one of the overexpressed lysosomal enzymes that is used for programmed drug release. In this regard, several enzyme-responsive gatekeepers such as cathepsin B sensitive peptides [54,55,56] and polyglutamic acid (PGA) [57] have been employed to mask the MSNs for on-demand drug release. Matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) [58,59], hyaluronidase [60,61], esterases [62,63], and trypsin [64] are other overexpressed enzymes, which can be applied as endogenous stimuli. The high bulk glucose concentration as an endogenous stimulus can also trigger the release of anti-cancer drugs. In this regard, it has been shown that use of functionalized MSNs with a biocompatible dextran gatekeeper led to glucose responsive drug release towards HeLa cells [65]. Numerous studies have confirmed the overexpression of adenosine triphosphate (ATP) in cancerous cells [66,67]. Therefore, having ATP-sensitive gatekeepers can accurately control the release of anti-cancer drugs. For instance, MSN-dependent ATP was synthesized by Zheng et al., and showed high ATP levels in cancer cells could successfully turn tunnels to "on" state and control the doxorubicin (DOX) release [68]. Similarly, a recent study by Bagheri et al. could block the MSNs surface by scrambled ATP aptamers as ATP-sensitive gatekeepers to accurately control the DOX release both in vitro and in vivo [69].

Temperature is among the most unique stimuli to control the release of therapeutic cargos via both endogenous and exogenous triggers. Some researchers have applied thermo-responsive gatekeepers based on the hyperthermic nature of TME enabling preferential drug release at the target site [70,71,72,73]. In addition, adding endogenous responsive agents such as phenylboronic acid and disulfide linkage to the backbone of MSNs can effectively destroy \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) and GSH in TME, thus indirectly elevating the temperature [74,75]. Consequently, thermo-responsive gatekeepers are decomposed, the pores become unbroken, and therapeutic cargos are released in a controllable fashion. Moreover, applying superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (SPIONs) inside the MSNs can effectively elevate the heat under external alternating magnetic field and improve the thermal response of gatekeepers [76,77,78]. Light irradiation has been proposed as a favorable strategy for creating an external stimulus triggering the release of therapeutic cargos, owing to the noninvasive feature and ability to manage it from outside. In this context, some MSNs have been developed based on light-responsive agents such as gold nanoparticles [79], carbon nanotubes [80], azobenzene [81,82] and reduced graphene oxide (RGO) [83,84] to control the release of anti-cancer drugs upon photo induction. Ultrasound (US) waves also represent promising exogenous strategies to control the release of the therapeutic molecules using mechanical effects. For instance, poly(dimethylsiloxane) (PDMS) [85] and tetrahydropyranyl methacrylate (THPMA) [86] were successfully grafted onto the MSNs as US gatekeepers to block the tunnels, hence, allowing the entrapped drug release upon the externally applied US. It has been shown that the application of external voltage stimulus can effectively

improve the release of therapeutic agents at a specific time and desired location. Therefore, electro-responsive gatekeepers such as chitosan hydrogel [87] and poly(3,4-ethylenedioxythiophene) doped with poly[(4-styrenesulfonic acid)-co-(maleic acid)] (PEDOT) [88] can be used for efficient drug release. MSNs can also be coupled with magnetic nanoparticles such as SPIONs to manage the drug release when exposed to an alternating magnetic field as exogenous trigger [89,90]. Despite the numerous advantages of MSNs, there are still some limitations, such as low stability and water solubility, that need to be overcome for any clinical applications [91]. Moreover, silica exists in great amounts in bone, cartilage, and tendons and has been categorized as "generally recognized as safe" (GRAS) by the FDA (U.S. Food and Drug Administration, ID Code: 14808-60-7). Recently, a new trial was carried out using silica nanoparticles, encapsulating fluorescent dyes and coated with polyethylene glycol (PEG), for stage I human colorectal cancer (ID Code: NCT02106598).

# 2.2. Superparamagnetic iron oxide nanoparticles (SPIONs)

SPIONs consisting of magnetite \(\left(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\right)\) or maghemite \(\left(\gamma\mathrm{-}\mathrm{Fe}_{2}0_{3}\right)\), are regarded as one of the greatest inorganic candidates for therapeutic applications because of their desirable biocompatibility [92], surface modification features, controlled drug delivery [93], low toxicity, and magnetic properties [94]. However, SPIONs suffer from low drug capacity due to the large mass of iron oxides, and thus high doses of them are needed to result toxicity in organisms. The ability to conjugate SPIONs with a variety of functional materials provides possible loading matrix for incorporation into therapeutic agents via covalent and noncovalent approaches. Drug molecules can be covalently conjugated to the surface of SPIONs with some polymers such as poly(aniline-co-sodium N-(1-one-butyric acid) aniline) (SPAnNa) [95] and poly[N-(1-one-butyric acid) aniline (SPAnH) [96]. In contrast, lauric acid (LA) [97], poly\(\gamma\)-glutamic acid (\(\gamma\)-PGM) [98] as well as chitosan (CS) [99] can be attached to the drug molecules noncovalently. Moreover, SPIONs as hyperthermia agents can be coupled with therapeutic cargos, and directly accumulate at the tumor region by applying an external magnetic field [100]. In this regard, Sadhukha et al., showed that magnetic hyperthermia treatment of lung cancer by targeted SPIONs results in significant tumor growth inhibition without any noticeable side effects [101]. In a similar experiment, it has been shown that SPIONs can induce hyperthermia, even in the presence of low magnetic field, leading to death of breast cancer cells [102]. Recently, in situ biosynthesis of SPIONs in combination with zinc gluconate was demonstrated in CRC cells following exposure to a magnetic field, which resulted in hyperthermia effect [103]. SPIONs can also be applied to track the cancer cells due to their unique magnetic properties with strong shortening effects under longitudinal (T1) and transverse (T2) relaxation [93]. A number of SPION agents have undergone various stages of clinical trials and some of them have received regulatory approvals in the United States of America (USA) and European Union (EU) as therapeutic and magnetic resonance imaging (MRI) contrast agents, which are different in coating materials, size, function, and in-vivo pharmacokinetic profiles (Fig. 3). Feraheme®/ferumoxytol is a form of SPIONs coated with polyglucose sorbitol carboxymethylether (PSC), which was approved by FDA in 2009 [104]. Ferumoxytol has received significant attention for hepatocellular imaging [105] and iron replacement therapy [106,107] due to its attractive characteristics, including its relatively small hydrodynamic diameter (\(\sim\!30\,\mathrm{nm}\)), low molecular weight, long half-life circulation (\(\sim\!15\,\mathrm{h}\)) [108] and unique safety profile [109]. Nanotherm®, aminosilane-coated SPIONs, are another applicable formula that has received the EU approval for use in magnetic hyperthermia treatment in patients with glioblastoma although
