# 2.3. Metal organic frameworks (MOFs)

Metal organic frameworks (MOFs) are a novel class of smart materials which are formed by different inorganic metal ions (e.g., iron and zinc) as the core and organic linkers (e.g., imidazolates and carboxylates) as the strut [117]. In recent years, MOFs have received attention as efficient nanocarriers for drug delivery applications due to several unique properties such as their porous structure, large surface area, easy chemical modifications [118], good biocompatibility [119], and low toxicity [120]. It has been demonstrated that MOFs can corporate with hydrogels to create elastic platforms for drug delivery applications [121]. Moreover, targeted manganese-based MOFs that are coated with silica have

shown extraordinarily high magnetic contrast for monitoring HT29 CRC cells [122]. It has been shown that, copper-based MOFs exhibit excellent photoacoustic imaging capability for tracking endogenous \(\mathrm{H}_{2}S\) levels in CRC and for evaluating the malignant behaviors [123]. So far, different classes of MOFs have been explored, which are summarized in Fig. 4 and we discuss the most important families in this section. Horcajada group reported the first family of non-toxic iron-based MOFs- (MIL-100 and MIL101) in 2008 that can be used in DDSs [124]. Since then, several types of MOFs with specific properties have been employed in tumor therapy and imaging. Moreover, the same group showed that iron based-MOFs are promising candidates to encapsulate a wide variety of hydrophobic and hydrophilic drugs and for theranostic applications. Interestingly, Horcajada et al. reported MIL88A can be employed as a contrast agent due to its favorable in vivo detection in Wistar female rats [118]. In this context, another study synthesized three different types of iron basedMOFs (MIL-53(Fe), MIL-88(Fe) and MIL-100(Fe)), and compared their applicability in anti-cancer drug delivery. Their results demonstrated that, MIL-100(Fe) has high carrying capacity, biocompatibility, and toxicity against breast cancer cells [125]. In contrast, another group reported that MIL-53 exhibited high drug loading capacity, controllable release performance, and effective cancer cell inhibition compared with HKUST-1 and MIL-101(Cr) nanocarriers [126]. Li et al., demonstrated that sorafenib-loaded MIL-101(Fe) effectively induced ferroptosis pathway in liver cancer cells. MRI results from xenograft mouse models showed that prepared nanocarriers had successfully accumulated in tumor mass [127]. Furthermore, a recent study carried out a comprehensive analysis to monitor and compare in vitro drug delivery by MIL-101(Cr) and UiO-66 nanocarriers. The results indicated that
