MIL-101(Cr) is a more favorable platform for DDSs compared with UiO-66 due to high loading capacity potential, rapid and sustained local drug release, great internalization, and more efficient cytotoxicity against breast cancer cells [128]. However, UiO-66 is considered as a type of zirconium-based MOFs with unique characteristics, which make it a great candidate for drug delivery applications. This family can be incorporated with contrast agents such as SPIONs for theranostic and tracking purposes [129,130]. Xue et al., prepared core–shell structure of SPION@UiO-66 and attached them to graphdiyne in order to make a 3D surface for high DOX loading. This nanocarrier exhibited greater tumor accumulation without notable side effects as compared with the free DOX [131]. It has been shown that the therapeutic efficacy of dichloroacetic acid (DCA) as an anti-cancer drug against MCF-7 breast cancer cells can be increased by more than 300 folds when loaded into coated-UiO-66 [132].

Zeolitic imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs) are one of the most interesting subclasses of the MOFs family as a drug delivery vehicle owing to their controllable drug release behavior under acidic conditions [133,134]. Moreover, the incorporation of ZIFs with bioimaging modalities such as \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}0_{4}\) [135,136,137], \([\mathsf{A s n}^{28}\) , \(\mathrm{Pro}^{30}\) , \(\mathrm{Trp}^{32}]\) -NPY [138]), and paramagnetic manganese (II) ions \((\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{+2})\) [139] not only deliver anti-cancer agents into tumor cells, but also provide better contrast when applied for MRI; thereby, they are suitable candidates for theranostic applications. Recently, the combination of chemotherapy and photothermal therapy (PTT) has

shown promising results with regard to improved therapeutic efficacy due to their synergistic effects [140]. In this context, ZIFs can be incorporated with photothermal agents, such as anisotropic AuNPs to create a multi-functional nanocarrier for successful realization of pH/NIR light irradiation dual-responsive drug delivery [141]. Recently, Guo group has developed new DDSs based on ZIFs combined with PDA as a photothermal agent and \(\mathsf{M}\mathsf{n}^{+2}\) as a contrast agent. Therefore, this multifunctional platform not only accelerated dual-responsive drug release but also facilitated cancer therapy with guided imaging [142]. Moreover, in addition to the anti-cancer drugs, delivery of other agents by ZIFs has been achieved in recent years. For example, Alsaiari et al. reported Cas9 protein delivery using ZIF-8 as a robust nanocarrier for transient gene editing [143]. It is important to evaluate the physicochemical properties of different classes of MOFs before their use as therapeutic options in biomedical applications [144].

