.

# 4. Implication of nanocarriers in colorectal cancer combination therapy

Combination of nanocarriers with multiple treatment modalities yields more significant therapeutic efficacy in patients, which is due to different pathway targeting, synergistic effects, improved therapeutic responses and reduced likelihood of damage to normal tissues [152,153]. Recently, numerous types of therapeutic combinations, such as PDT/immunotherapy or active targeting, PTT/active targeting, RT-RDT/immunotherapy, RT/immunotherapy, hyperthermia/chemotherapy, and ligand-based strategy/chemotherapy have been examined for CRC therapy both in vitro and in vivo based on MSNs, SPIONs, and MOFs. Some examples of using nanocarriers in combination therapy for the treatment of CRC are summarized in Table 3. For instance, combining PDT and an immune checkpoint blockade approach endowed unique synergistic mechanisms for local and distant CRC eradication [154,155]. It has been demonstrated that selective delivery of MOFs at the tumor site can be facilitated by introducing hyaluronic acid (HA) moiety targeting, which resulted in maximum CRC inhibition in response to PDT [146]. Similarly, conjugating targeting agent of epithelial cell molecule mucin 1 (MUC1) aptamer onto Au@SPIONs led to high accumulation of nanocarriers at the desired location and more efficient PTT of colon cancer [156]. The durable treatment response rates and CRC regression have also been reported in the combination of RT-RDT and immunotherapy through Hf-based MOFs [157,158]. Similarly, synergistic combination of MOF-mediated RT and \(\alpha\)-PD-L1 antibody as immune checkpoint inhibitor provided impressive opportunity for CRC inhibition in mouse models [159,160]. Hyperthermia is another therapeutic strategy to induce malignant tumor regression when used in combination with chemotherapy. In this regard, Eynali et al., used core-shell NPs, SPIONs@poly lactic-co-glycolic
