# 5. Different administration routes of nanocarriers

Selection of a feasible route of drug administration is considered as an important factor in therapeutic strategies, which can influence the pharmacokinetics, distribution, and retention of nanocarriers. Therefore, suitable strategies based on type of cancer, drugs and their mechanism of action provide not only patient convenience, but also facilitate better therapeutic effectiveness [165]. The oral and intravenous routes of drug administration are the common choices for introducing chemotherapeutic drugs to the body [166] and will be discussed in the following sections in more details.

# 5.1. Oral route of administration

Although the oral route is the most preferred method for drug administration owing to its non-invasive nature, pain avoidance, self-administration, patient's acquiescence, cost-effectiveness and simplicity, it can also present several major problems such as physiological barriers, digestive enzymes, environmental pH, and rapid drug excretion from the body; solubility and permeability of drugs play an important role in their absorption rates [167,168,169]. The efficient surface engineering of DDSs may enhance drug stability and bioavailability and thus overcome oral drug delivery challenges. In this regard, several researchers have conducted experiments to improve oral formulations based on nanotechnology for CRC specific drug delivery. In this context, Tian et al., designed an appropriate pH-responsive MSN for oral administration, which can effectively release anticancer drugs in colon tissue with weak basic environment (pH = 7.0–8.0) [41]. Moreover, Hidalgo et al. reported a strategy for the first time to protect the nanocarrier from enzymatic degradation and enhance cellular uptake without toxicity, by coating the MIL-100(Fe) with CS biopolymer [170]. Similar to Hidalgo et al., other groups have also prepared suitable oral MOF-carriers (MOF-5 and UiO-66) with hydrogel beads and provided a better protection against stomach acidic conditions [171,172]. Moreover, it has been shown that oral drug delivery can be tuned by surface functionalization of MSN with PEI and PEG which resulted in enhancement of the drug internalization to small intestine and colon by PEI and PEG-PEI coatings, respectively [173].

# 5.2. Intravenous route of administration

Intravenous administration is another way to distribute therapeutic agents throughout the bloodstream; however it is limited
noma antigen in colon cancer [192]. EpCAM is a cell surface marker, which is highly expressed in different types of epithelial tissues and is over-expressed in a wide variety of human malignancies such as colorectal cancer [193,194]. It has been shown that EpCAM is involved in cancer proliferation [195], and invasion [196], thus it can be accounted as an ideal therapeutic target in colorectal cancer treatment. Xie et al. conjugated for the first time EpCAM aptamer with MSN for targeted delivery of DOX to SW620 colon cancer cells. They suggested that targeted delivery of therapeutic agents into EpCAM positive colon cancer cells can enhance the anti-cancer efficacy of the drug while minimizing the side effects [175]. Similarly, Li et al. decorated MSNs with EpCAM aptamer to reduce severe side effects of DM1 (a cytotoxic agent derived from maytansine) and demonstrated that the binding ability of nanocarrier increases after conjugation with aptamer both in vitro and in vivo [197]. Multi-targeting techniques can be considered as another strategy to achieve more effective tumor therapy. In this regard, the same research group developed novel multifunctional MSNs to deliver miR-328 by conjugating EpCAM aptamer with Bev antibody, which targeted the EpCAM biomarker and vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) receptor of CRC cells, respectively. They indicated that multiple surface marker approach can be used as a better option in gene silencing, CSCs inhibition and tumor eradication [44].

CD44, a transmembrane glycoprotein, is a common biomarker for CSCs in several human malignancies such as colorectal cancer [198]. Interestingly, many studies reported that elevated expression of CD44 acts as a tumor promoter in CRC [199,200]. In this regard, Yu et al. enhanced DOX internalization in HCT116 cells via MSN, which was decorated with HA [201]. Another group showed the potential of MSN functionalized with HA can improve the bioavailability and anti-tumor effects of DOX in a xenograft mouse model [174]. CD133 is another important CSC marker, which is overexpressed on the cell surface of various human malignancies such as CRC and is associated with tumorigenesis [202,203]. Moreover, Zahiri et al. increased DOX internalization in CSCs by developing MSNs which were tagged with an RNA aptamer against CD133 overexpression CSCs [204]. Moreover, there are some pluripotency factors, also known as intracellular biomarkers of CSCs, such as SOX2 [205], OCT4 [206,207], and NANOG [208,209]which are aberrantly expressed in CRC cells involved in chemotherapy resistance, metastasis, and poor survival.

Although cell surface receptors have been established as effective markers for drug delivery into target cells, it is thought that, direct delivery of drugs to specific subcellular compartments such as neucleus, mitochondrion, endoplasmic reticulum (ER) and Golgi complex is another strategy in DDSs which should be considered in eradicating cancer. It is important to know the intracellular targets of various chemotherapeutic agents to improve their availability in respective organelles. For instance, some of the anti-cancer drugs such as DOX and dichloroacetate (DCA) were localized in the nuclei and mitochondria of cancer cells, respectively [210,211]. These organelle specific targeting strategies could maximize the therapeutic effects, minimize the adverse side effects and address MDR problems. As of yet, nanocarriers such as MSNs, SPIONs, and MOFs have not been designed to deliver chemotherapeutic drugs to specific organelles within CRC cells, the nanocarriers that are employed in other cancers are listed in Table 5.

# 5.3. Challenges in the design of nanocarriers

Despite numerous benefits of using nanocarriers, there are some challenges associated with them that should be considered in their design process, and can be divided into two groups:

1) Some challenges are related to the preparation of nanocarriers in laboratory that could highly impact their physicochemical characteristics such as drug loading capacity, type of surface coating, size, and shape. 2) Other challenges are associated with in vivo behavior of nanocarriers including their biodistribution, toxicity, fate or exertion, release of anti-cancer agents, and receptor-targeted drug delivery as well as the nature of TME, which are required to be well-defined. • Drug loading capacity

Control over the pore size and volume can directly impact the loading capacity and the release rate of therapeutic agents. Nanocarriers with larger pore volumes tend to have greater loading capacity and higher amounts of cargoes can be encapsulated inside the pores [212]. Several studies have been carried out to overcome the small pore size and to increase the loading capacity, since this characteristic determines required doses of therapeutic agents and the frequency of their administration. Furthermore, it has been demonstrated that based on the type and amount of surfactant used, as well as synthetic condition of preparation, different degree of porosity can be achieved. In this regard, Ganguly et al., showed that using the tetraethyl ortho silicate (TEOS) in the presence of PEG, as a long chain surfactant, results in the formation of MSNs with large-sized pores [213]. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that application of a surfactant with a longer hydrocarbon chain, such as octylammonium bromide (OTAB), leads to smaller MSN size with larger pore diameters compared with cetyltrimethylammonium chloride (CTAC) and tosylate ion (CTATOS) surfactants [214]. Another study, which was conducted in MSNs, exhibited that the reaction conditions are altered by varying the amount of cetrimonium bromide (CTAB), \(\mathrm{NH}_{3}/\mathrm{TEOS}\), and molar ratio of water/TEOS. Results indicated that an increase in the amount of water causes a larger specific surface area while the pore size is kept in a constant range [215]. In a recent study, Sheykholeslami et al., investigated different surfactant concentrations (4.6, 5.9 and \(7.2\;\mathrm{mM}\)) on the specific surface area and the total pore volume. Their results indicated that the increase in the specific surface area of MSNs is obtained at \(7.2\;\mathrm{mM}\) CTAB concentration while the mean pore volume decreased and maximum pore was determined at \(5.9\;\mathrm{mM}\) CTAB [216]. Generally, different types of surfactants such as CTAB [217,218,219], an ionic liquid \(/ \mathrm{CO}_{2} / [220]\), and combination of CTAB and amino acid [221] are employed in the synthesis of MOFs. However, it is still a major challenge to prepare the MOFs with tunable and tailored porosity structures. Recently, Duan et al., used four surfactants with different alkyl chain lengths to synthesize MOFs with desirable pore size. They also revealed that, by increasing the alkyl chain length groups (C12 to C18) of the employed surfactant, the size of pores can be adjusted from 3.61 to \(4.88\;\mathrm{nm}\) [222]. It seems that by shifting the coordination sites of ligands in MOFs, the pore size can be adjusted [223].

# • Coating, size, shape, toxicity and fate

Surface coating is thought to be another important factor that must be kept in mind during synthesis process. The surface of nanocarriers can be coated by addition of hydrophobic and hydrophilic polymers to improve their colloidal stability, reduce plasma protein binding, and enhance their biocompatibility, life span, immune escape, and intracellular uptake [224,225]. To achieve these goals, the outer surface modification of nanocarriers should preserve their structure, porosity, and release abilities. In this context, it has been shown that CD molecules are more suitable than PEG for this purpose due to the maintained structure and porosity of MOFs. Presumably, pores of MOFs can be blocked by the PEG chains resulting in the reduction of the surface area [226]. To over-
come this drawback, Bellido and coworkers described a new method to modify the outer surface of MIL-100 with the biocompatible heparin polymer that preserves the physicochemical features of the nanocarrier [227]. Another study reported that silica shells can preserve the MIL-101 porosity, control the release of the encapsulated drugs, and prevent its rapid degradation, albeit causing new toxicity issues [228]. The major challenge associated with surface modification is alteration in the surface charge of nanocarriers and their toxicity. Toxicity of nanocarriers is a critical issue, which needs to be considered before their widespread use in drug delivery and clinical applications. In this regard, Berry et al. reported that, dextran coating on the surface of SPIONs resulted in a toxic effect [229]. In contrast, another group clearly showed that dextran, as a safe polysaccharide coating, plays a critical role in the stability of SPIONs [230]. Moreover, silica-coated SPIONs have been investigated in mice without any significant toxicity on major organs [231]. It has been shown that the applied coating of SPIONs with a single layer of MSNs not only increases the surface area for anti-cancer drug loading, but also preserves the magnetic properties efficiently, compared with two layers silica coating [232]. Further, covalent cross-linking of polyvinyl alcohol (PVA) to SPIONs adjusted their surface charge and also made them nonimmunogenic and nontoxic to immune cells [233]. It should be noted that, an increase in the level of iron at the tumor site can act as a cofactor, and potentially, promote cancer proliferation [234]. Therefore, surface modification of SPIONs improves their colloidal stability, prevents aggregation, preserves magnetic properties and avoids the possible release of iron [235]. In general, it has been shown that nanocarriers, with a positive surface charge such as CS, have significant toxicity [236], compared to those with neutral and negative surface charges such as PEG [237,238]. Feng et al. investigated the in vivo behavior and toxicity of SPIONs with two different surface coatings (PEG and polyethylene imine (PEI)) and sizes (10 and \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\)) in mice. They indicated that, the \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\) PEG coated SPIONs, showed higher tumor uptake and relatively slower clearance without obvious cytotoxic effects compared with the \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) PEI-coating [239]. Therefore, controlling the particle size of prepared nanocarriers is another critical parameter to be considered in biomedical applications, which has a profound influence on the half-life of nanocarriers in the circulation. In this regard, it has been demonstrated that in vivo toxicity of MSNs is significantly associated with their porosity and size, which can cause vascular obstruction due to the interaction of NPs with serum proteins and, consequently changing the size of serum hydrodynamic [240]. Moreover, it has been reported that NPs smaller than \(10\,\mathrm{nm}\) in diameter are generally excreted through renal clearance, while those larger than \(100\,\mathrm{nm}\) tend to have a higher accumulation in the spleen and liver [241]. Fu et al., performed a comprehensive study to evaluate biodistribution, excretion, and toxicity of MSNs with \(110\,\mathrm{nm}\) size after four different routes of administration. Their results indicated that MSNs could be entrapped in the liver with low absorption rate through intramuscular and hypodermic administration. Oral administration of MSNs led to absorption into the intestinal tract and liver accumulation. Exposed by intravenous administration, MSNs were mostly persisted in the liver and spleen. Furthermore, it was also shown that, MSNs were mainly removed through urine and feces following the four different administration routes [242]. Another study was carried out by Batti and colleagues to evaluate the toxicity and biodistribution of three different MOFs (MIL-88A, MIL-100 and MIL-88B_4CH3) after intravenous administration in Wistar rats. Their results indicated that the smaller nanoparticle (MIL-88B_4CH3) could easily bypass the blood brain barrier (BBB) without severe cerebral toxicity [120]. Moreover, long-term toxicity studies of MOF nanodots showed fast renal clearance with low systemic toxicity [243]. Furthermore, Zhang et al. demonstrated that \(\mathrm{Fe}_{3}\mathrm{O}_{4}@\mathrm{C@PMN}\) can be completely

removed from the body through feces after 8 days without any noticeable toxicity for the main organs [244]. He et al. investigated the biodistribution and excretion of different sizes (80, 120, 200, and \(360\,\mathrm{nm}\)) of MSNs in mice. They showed that larger particles can easily be trapped by spleen and liver following intravenous administration, which results in their decreased blood-circulation lifetime [245]. Moreover, the iron-core in FeOOH/MSN-PEG could better enhance the particle degradation compared with MSN-PEG in both in vitro and in vivo conditions. These results can be justified by the presence of iron (III) as a biological ligand in the mammalian serum degradation medium, which can bind to transferrin protein with high affinity [246]. Furthermore, the morphology of NPs also affects their in vivo behavior and environmental fate. For instance, long-rod MSNs have been found to be trapped in the spleen, whereas, short-rod MSNs can be accumulated in the liver. Moreover, long-rod MSNs showed a longer blood circulation time compared with the short-rod and spherical MSNs [247,248]. It is generally believed that the fate of MSNs, is relatively independent of the particle size and is mainly related to the particle composition and conditions used in their synthesis [249].

# Release of anti-cancer agents

The controlled release capacity of therapeutic cargos within the target tumor site in an on-demand manner can be achieved by designing internal and external stimuli responsive gatekeepers for the end-capping of MSNs, which is discussed in section 2–1. According to the specific properties of tumor cells, researchers have focused on the dual or multimodal stimuli-responsive nanocarriers to improve drug delivery performance. For example, Xiao et al., developed a pH- and redox- dual-responsive MSN for tumor-triggered DOX delivery in U87 glioblastoma cells [250]. Tran et al., have also reported dual stimulus (pH/NIR) responsive core–shell MSNs for improved cisplatin release behavior. They showed the enhancement of release rate by changing pH from 7.4 to 5.5 under NIR irradiation (808 nm, \(1.5\,\mathrm{W/cm}^{2}\)) for \(10\,\mathrm{min}\) [251]. Additionally, Yan et al., fabricated pH/redox-triggered MSNs for codelivery of DOX/paclitaxel (PTX) in BT549 breast cancer cell lines [252]. Moreover, Wen et al., designed triple-control release MSNs-platform based on stimulus-responsive GSH/pH/light for enhanced release efficiency [253]. Another group prepared pH/redox/NIR as an effective multimodal response system based on MSNs for synergistic chemo-photothermal antitumor therapy [254]. Furthermore, Wang et al., designed novel quadruple-responsive nanocarriers based on RGO/MSNs to control the release of the loaded rhodamine B under pH, temperature, NIR light, and redox stimuli [255]. Grading the specific responsive linkers to SPIONs is one of the feasible methods to control the release of anti-cancer drugs. Zhu et al., prepared multifunctional pH-sensitive SPIONs by conjugating acylhydrazone linkers to overcome the unspecific drug release [256]. In another similar study phenylboronic acid (PBA) was used for effective release of DOX from SPIONs in acidic conditions [257]. Lee et al., synthesized a pH/enzyme dependent release system with a tetrapeptide (GFLG) linker, which could be cleaved by a lysosomal enzyme. The prepared nanocarrier could effectively prevent enzymatic degradation and release the trapped drugs in the presence of dual stimuli [258]. Moreover, poly(2-isopropyl-2-oxazoline) (PIPOX) and tannic acid (TA) were also used to prepare thermo/pH responsive SPIONs [259]. It is worth noting that multiple subclasses of MOFs such as MIL-100(Fe) [260], ZNF-8 [261,262], and UiO-66 [263] have been proposed to have inherent pH-responsiveness. In general, different types of functional groups, which exist on surface of the MOFs, provide a flexible structure to form covalent bonds with functional molecules. Conjugation of stimuli-responsive polymers to the MOFs through post-synthetic modifications (PSMs) is a promising
strategy that reduces the risk of premature therapeutic cargos leakage while optimizing the drug release performance. In addition to pH-responsive, other stimuli have been recommended for controlled drug release of MOFs through PSMs including redox [264,265,266,267], temperature [268,269,270,271], magnetic [272], pressure [273], ATP [274,275], and humidity-responsiveness [276]. Employing dual stimuli-responsive MOFs can improve the efficiency of therapy. Wang et al., covalently linked benzoic imine and redox responsive disulfide bonds to MIL-101 during the modification process and achieved the controlled DOX release by dual pH/redox response [277]. Moreover, other groups fabricated MIL-100 and ZIF-8 platform based on dual pH/NIP stimuli to control and sustain the drug release in cancer cells [278,141]. The Jiang group developed dual ATP/pH responsive constructs to generate on-demand release of nanocarriers. According to their results, due to the synergistic effects of both stimuli, DOX could be released more effectively from ZIF-90 inside the tumor cells [279]. Moreover, Nagata et al., employed acrylic acid (AA) and poly(N-isopropylacrylamide) (PNIPAM) to prepare the dual pH/thermo responsive UiO-66 for the first time [280].

# • Receptor-mediated drug delivery

As mentioned earlier, there are various receptors overexpressed in CRC cells which make them useful for active targeting. However, it is important to consider the receptor profiling, expression level, variation of receptor expression, distribution pattern of receptors and receptor density for each patient in order to personalize therapy and to choose more effective strategies based on DDSs, which could save the patients from medicinal costs. Furthermore, based on age, sex and stage of cancer, case-by-case quantitative and qualitative analyses through various tests should be performed to select more suitable DDSs for personalized therapy and to predict the effectiveness of treatment strategy. In addition, for more efficient CSC eradication, combination of targeting CSCs associated with intracellular markers (such as SCX2, OCT4 and NANOG pluripotency factors) and surface biomarkers could be a novel strategy to increase on-target/off-tumor toxicity, prevent the reemergence of cancers, and obtain optimal output.

# • Tumor microenvironment (TME)

TME is the fundamental drug delivery barrier, which requires particular attention. As mentioned before, nanocarriers could passively accumulate at the lesion area by the EPR effect, but sometimes minimal therapeutic effect is observed due to poor nanocarrier penetration into the deeper areas. A recent metaanalysis of NP delivery to the tumor sites concluded that the mean and median delivery efficiencies of NPs decreased from \(2.24\%\) and \(0.76\%\) injected dose (ID) at \(24\,\mathrm{h}\) to \(1.23\%\) and \(0.35\%\) ID at \(168\,\mathrm{h}\), respectively, due to the critical barriers in solid tumors [281]. Therefore, applying innovative strategies based on DDSs may be useful to address this major obstacle, one of them taking part in ECM degradation (Fig. 5). For example, it has been demonstrated that the functionalized surface of MSNs with digestive enzymes such as bromelain [282], pepsin [283], collagenase [284], and MMP-2 sensitive peptides [47] acts against the rigid ECM and leads to cleared pathways for drug diffusion into the deeper parts of solid tumors. These results are from the study conducted by Dong et al., in an effort to deplete the collagen in tumor matrix for facilitating both MSNs and DOX penetration into breast tumors. MSNs were loaded with NO to activate MMP-1 and \(^{-2}\) expressions, which resulted in disintegrated collagen and improved antitumor efficacy 2.7-folds more than NO-free nanocarriers [285]. Another recent study showed that disrupting the TME with collagenase treatment prior to intraperitoneal (IP) delivery of nanocarriers provided

better PEGylated-MSNs distribution into OVCAR-8 3D tumor spheroids [286]. Vascular disruption of TME has resulted in blood supply reduction and tumor necrosis. Mulens-Arias et al. demonstrated that the PEI-coated SPIONs with intrinsic capacity are accumulated in the TME leading to impaired vascular endothelial cell migration in breast cancer xenograft model due to the antiangiogenic potential of nanocarriers [287]. Manipulating hypoxia is another strategy that has attracted attention for TME modulation. It has been demonstrated that delivering nanocarriers such as Hf-based MOFs [155,158], \(\mathrm{Mn}_{2}0_{4}\) derived from Mn-MOF [288], TBD-Pt (IV)@MOF-199 [289], ZDOP@PP [290] and Cu-based MOF-199 [291] into tumors led to in situ decomposition of \(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}\) to \(0_{2}\) and improving the PDT efficacy and TME modification. Besides, reshaping the immune microenvironment is a way to enhance systemic antitumor immune responses for tumor rejection. Indoleamine 2,3-dioxygenase (IDO) is one of the immunosuppressive factors which has a high expression in TME, leading to poor treatment outcomes. It has been shown that the combination of IDO inhibitor-loaded MOFs with X-ray treatment or RT/RDT is an effective strategy to generate systemic anti-tumor immunity to suppress the CRC [154,157]. Moreover, the combination of powerful platform of Hf-based MOFs with RT/PDT and anti-programmed death ligand 1 (PD-L1) as an immune checkpoint inhibitor can effectively suppress TME by reactivating antitumor immunity to eradicate lung metastasis [159,158,160]. Moreover, due to an intrinsic therapeutic effect of a FDA-approved SPION, ferumoxytol, it could be applied as off label to inhibit tumor growth. Results have shown that ferumoxytol induces pro-inflammatory macrophages in the TME and avoids liver metastatic [292]. Making nanocarriers with cell membranes, referred as biomimetic strategy, could reduce their recognition by the immune system, mimic the interaction between stromal cells and tumor cells, and then increase their intensity in the tumor mass. Zhang et al., showed that MSNs could be easily recruited by the CT-26 tumor cells because of the coated platelet membrane (PM) which could lead to significant in vivo apoptosis rate compared with other experimental groups [293]. Another similar study was developed by Illes et al. demonstrating that coating MIL-88A with exosome derived from HeLa cells created promising DDSs that facilitate longer circulation times and probably promote tumor penetration [294]. Furthermore, Zhang et al., grafted erythrocyte membrane onto the ZIF-8 that was co-loaded with glucose oxidase (GOx) and tirapazamine (TPZ) for CRC treatment. Their results showed more effective tumor suppression with an intelligent biomimetic platform in a safe manner [295]. Another biomimetic platform was constructed by Li and colleagues that coated Pt(II) porphyrinic-MOF nanocarriers with breast cancer cell membranes for efficient local cancer therapy and immune escape purposes [296]. Recently, it has been shown that the in situ self-assembly of SPIONs in the TME can be considered as a promising strategy to access deep tumor sites and to eradicate cancer cells more effectively [103]. Moreover, it has been demonstrated that, the gap between vascular endothelial cells in TME is about \(200\,\mathrm{nm}\) and nanocarriers greater than \(100\,\mathrm{nm}\) cannot penetrate within tumors. On the other hand, nanocarriers smaller than \(30\,\mathrm{nm}\) can penetrate into deeper sites, but they are quickly eliminated through bloodstream [297]. To overcome this dilemma, size-shrinkable MSN/MOF-based platforms have been designed to improve the penetration capacity. In this context, He et al., engineered a size switchable, ball-and-rod nanocarrier for long circulation and effective tumor penetration porposes. Smaller sizes of MSNs \((40\,\mathrm{nm})\) were synthesized and connected using a peptidic sequence (GPLGIAGQ) cleavable with MMP-2 to make one large size \((100\,\mathrm{nm})\). Upon reaching at TME, ball-and-rod nanocarrier was dissociated and small size MSNs were released to enhance tumor penetration [298].
