5.4. Preclinical models: Promising platforms for passing barriers

Many studies need to be conducted on different DDSs to confirm their safety and evaluate some other important factors such as cost and the possibility for clinical or marketing applications. In this context, carrying out preclinical researches including mathematical modeling, using in vitro high-throughput platforms and choosing appropriate animal models are essential approaches for assessing nanocarriers and their translation into the clinic. Nanoinformatics has provided a valuable platform as an alternative lab model for analyzing and processing information about the physicochemical characteristics of nanocarriers, their safety, nanocarrier–tumor cell interactions, binding affinity and drug delivery efficacy [299,300]. Wang et al., developed spatio-temporal mathematical models to evaluate the drug delivery via MSNs and help estimating the tumor response prior to treatment [301]. Brocato et al., used mathematical models to predict the in vivo treatment outcome of MSNs by assessing the nanocarriers biodistribution without invasive methods and animal sacrifice [302]. Another group applied the imaging and mathematical modeling techniques to determine the effects of size, surface charge, coating, and route of administration on biodistribution and clearance of MSNs in healthy rats. Their results indicated that \(32\mathrm{-nm}\) trimethylsilane (TMS)-alkylated MSNs and \(56\mathrm{-nm}\) quaternary amine (QA)-

aminated MSNs are better choices for therapeutic applications owing to their low accumulation in healthy tissues [303]. Moreover, recent studies have evaluated the flow and interaction of iron oxide nanoparticles as a DDS model by classical computational fluid dynamics (CFD) simulations together with experimentation to achieve comprehensive insights of the transport mechanisms [304,305]. Erucar and Keskin compared the storage and release of drugs in MOFs (MIL-5B(Fe), MIL-100(Fe), MIL-101(Cr), and zeolites), and MSNs using molecular simulations. Their results demonstrated that MOFs could be considered as strong nanocarriers due to high loading potential and slow diffusion [306]. They further selected MOF-74 to compare the storage and delivery of two anticancer drugs, methotrexate (MTX) and 5-FU, with computational investigation. They showed that 5-FU possesses a better adsorption owing to higher entropic effects [307]. Shahabi and Raissi evaluated the drug delivery performance of MOF in the presence of external electric field by molecular dynamics (MD) simulations. They showed that weak drug interaction with MOF under electric field leads to higher dynamic movements and more diffusion of drug molecules [308]. For better outcomes during clinical trials and more effective CRC therapy, it is necessary to simulate the TME. In this context, Kather et al., generated in silico models based on cellular interactions and showed that activating adaptive immune system and stromal depletion in TME correlate with CRC
